TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan (Launch Specifics, Orbit Specifics)"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan, first paragraph"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Figure 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Table 2"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress, Table 3"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress, Table 3","issue":"Inclination of 97.8° is stated without showing the J2 nodal precession calculation that produces it."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress, Table 3","issue":"6000 pixels across-track is assumed without justification; the 60 km swath and hence the coverage conclusion depend on it."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress, interpretation","issue":"Track spacing of 2,700 km is equatorial; at ~20°S latitude it is ~2,540 km, and the conclusion that weekly coverage fails is understated rather than overstated."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"'30 percent more acidic' is imprecise; ocean pH has fallen ~0.1 (≈30% rise in H+ concentration)."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale, objective 2","issue":"10 m GSD is to be 'refined against the method in [4]', but [4] is a news article, not the primary method paper, and no derivation is given."},{"where":"References [6]","issue":"Cited to support BoM SST fusion, but the source is a general NOAA page that does not address this."}],"overall":"Strong, well-structured preliminary report: a well-motivated Australian reef-bleaching mission with quantified objectives, users, a justified Gantt plan with contingencies and sources, and correct hand calculations that expose the key coverage-versus-resolution trade. Weaknesses are secondary sourcing and some unjustified assumptions.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"Clear goal, four quantified objectives, named users with needs explicitly mapped to objectives, and literature support [1]-[6]. Minor weaknesses: some sources are secondary/news items and the 10 m GSD is asserted rather than derived.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef, so that bleaching is detected within days"},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"The satellite would be operated by the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS). Its customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"Gantt chart with task descriptions, risk/contingency table, and a source table. Plan is explicitly justified against the mission narrative and includes float and fallbacks. Slightly generic task descriptions in places.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"The order follows the mission: the imaging requirements (objectives 1 and 2) fix the orbit altitude and local time"},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Week 10 is float as well as editing time: no new design work is planned in it."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Hand calculations for a, T, orbits/day, inclination, track spacing and swath are correct and interpreted, identifying the key coverage trade. Limited to one orbit case; no link budget or coverage simulation results yet.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"Weekly coverage (objective 1) is therefore not guaranteed by one satellite at this altitude and resolution."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Logical structure, consistent headings, labelled figure and tables, in-text citations matching the reference list. Some references are weak (news article, ResearchGate entry without authors) and [6] does not really support the BoM claim.","evidence":[{"where":"Figure 1","quote":"Figure 1: Project Gantt chart, Weeks 2 to 10 (gold = completed, blue = planned, red lines = report deadlines)."},{"where":"References","quote":"[7] J. R. Wertz, D. F. Everett, and J. J. Puschell, Eds., Space Mission Engineering: The New SMAD."}]}]}
